---

## Python library contract

`src/geocebada/` is the reusable library used by notebooks, experiments and the web app. Stable logic must live there rather than being copied between notebooks or embedded in Streamlit callbacks.

Current public utilities include:

- project/data/source path resolution and YAML config loading;
- file discovery and multi-CSV concatenation;
- declarative DataFrame filtering for categorical, numeric-range and text filters;
- official yield split loading, validation, partitioning and safe metadata attachment;
- climate GeoTIFF discovery, filename parsing, inventory and metadata;
- CRS validation/reprojection safeguards;
- official parcel-geometry loading without CRS guessing;
- generic parcel-level aggregation for exploratory mapping;
- declarative grouped/temporal feature recipes (`mean`, `max`, `slope`, `auc`, etc.);
- feature-table joins;
- Pearson/Spearman association tests;
- multiple-testing correction (Benjamini-Hochberg/FDR, Holm, Bonferroni);
- feature screening and train-vs-prediction covariate-shift screening;
- linear-regression diagnostics including residual metrics, Shapiro-Wilk, Breusch-Pagan and VIF;
- reusable Plotly distribution, scatter, pairplot, correlation-heatmap, residual and parcel-map figures;
- univariate IQR outlier summaries;
- exploratory regression benchmarks using identical shuffled K-fold splits.

Before writing a new helper:

1. search `docs/FUNCTION_INDEX.md`;
2. inspect the relevant module under `src/geocebada/`;
3. extend an existing function/module when that is the same responsibility;
4. create a new function only when responsibility is genuinely distinct.

After adding/renaming/removing a public function or class, run:

```bash
python tools/generate_function_index.py
```

Public reusable code should have type hints, docstrings, explicit assumptions and tests once behavior is stable. Keep notebook-only exploratory snippets out of the public API.

